    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The theoretical landscape of Social Capital is highly contested, evolving from early resource-based views to sophisticated analyses of social structures. Early conceptualizations, notably by Coleman and Bourdieu, distinguished between different forms of capital, with Bourdieu emphasizing institutionalized forms and Coleman focusing on familial trust. Granovetter's seminal work highlighted the practical utility of weak ties, contrasting with Lin's focus on structural connections within networks. Further refinement includes Portes and Putnam, who examine how social networks impact economic outcomes and civic life, respectively. Contemporary research, incorporating the insights of Adler, Burt, and Waldinger, delves into network density and structural holes, while Fukuyama and Loury address broader societal implications of trust and social cohesion.",
